(Phnom Penh): The Conference on "Resilient Cities: Reflecting on Challenges and Urban Reconstruction" of the 39th General Assembly of the International Community of French-Speaking Mayors (AIMF) was held at the Sokha Residence Hotel. Phnom Penh this morning, December 3, 2019, welcomed 402 delegates from cities and sub-national administrative associations from 41 countries around the world.
Mr. Khuong Sreng stated that the 39th General Assembly of the International Association of French Mayors is a historic event for AIMF in general and in particular for Phnom Penh to strengthen and expand deeper and broader cooperation. Within the framework of cities with associations and cities with cities that are members of the International Association of Mayors use French.
He affirmed that Phnom Penh is very honored and proud to host the 39th General Assembly of the International Association of French-Speaking Mayors with the official visit of a delegation of cities and sub-national administrative associations. 402 came from 41 countries around the world.
Mr. Khuong Sreng said that during the 40 years of Phnom Penh, the management of Phnom Penh has developed in 5 major stages: from 1979 to 1993, the management of Phnom Penh played a role for the sake of peace, acceptance Repatriation and opposition to the return of the genocidal regime.
From 1993 to 2000, the administration of Phnom Penh played a viable role in maintaining political stability and restoring the city's physical infrastructure. From 2000 to 2008, the management of Phnom Penh played an implementation role for the cause of internal security, continuing to rehabilitate and build the city's physical infrastructure.
From 2008 to 2013, the management of Phnom Penh played an active role in strengthening security, public order, continuing to rebuild the city's physical infrastructure and developing poor communities. From 2013 to the present, the management of Phnom Penh plays an active role for the cause of reform, sustainable development and the future of Smart City.
Pierre Byrne, secretary of the International Association of French-Speaking Governors, said rebuilding the city requires the ability of the people and the leadership to overcome the effects of urban change and any shocking situation. But resilience to these situations is not something that happens by itself, it comes and goes with the help of public policy, social and economic development programs, or mass activities.
These initiatives lead to a reduction in social divisions and a direct response to the waiting of the most vulnerable people. Equitable urban development with the participation of the people is an indispensable condition for ensuring sustainable urban reconstruction.
It is in this sense that the workshop was set up specifically to focus on rebuilding cities after major disasters, conflicts or natural disasters. The interventions cited at this time focus on three vital energy factors that are essential for reconstruction, and are closely linked to the three phases in which people and cities are separated.
The workshop "Urban resilience, taking into account the challenges of urban reconstruction" in collaboration with the Center for Khmer Studies, the Institute of Urban Planning, Paris, the Association for Training and Research on Urban Planning and Urban Planning at Sokha Hotel.
Minister Veng Sakhon: The Royal Government considers the fight against human trafficking as a priority that needs to be addressed
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, on December 3, 2019, presided over the opening ceremony of the dissemination workshop on "Prevention and Combating Human Trafficking in Agriculture" organized by the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries. Held at the Royal University of Agriculture.
The event was attended by the Secretary of State, Undersecretary of State, Ms. Chou Bun Eng, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior and Permanent Vice President of the National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking, professors, teachers, civil servants, staff and 750 students.
Minister Veng Sakhon praised the efforts of all stakeholders to co-organize this workshop well and especially the achievements that the working group has achieved in relation to the prevention and fight against human trafficking in agriculture.
Minister Veng Sakhon stated that "the Royal Government has made the fight against human trafficking a priority that needs to be addressed and promoted through human resources, budget and operational materials."
At the same time, the Minister of Agriculture also said that the Ministry has cooperated in implementing the National Strategic Plan to Combat Human Trafficking 2019-2023 to contribute to improving the effectiveness of the implementation of government policies.
Minister Veng Sakhon also provided five key recommendations to stakeholders as a basis for implementation, such as:
First, to increase cooperation with the National Committee for the Dissemination of Laws, Key National Policies, International Conventions, National Action Plans, the ASEAN Convention on Combating Human Trafficking and the bilateral and multilateral regional and international action plans.
Second, all agricultural educational institutions must work together to educate young people and students to understand the principles of the law against human trafficking.
Third, to cooperate with the competent authorities to report crimes that occur in the local farmers related to human trafficking, labor exploitation, sex trafficking, drug trafficking.
Fourth, we must continue our efforts to create jobs in both quantity and quality, especially for the youth, by providing technical skills, providing more convenient agricultural services to farmers, including commune outreach agencies, in order to reduce migration and provide broader cooperation. And encourage the participation and support of all stakeholders in a "public-private-development and community" approach.
Fifth, request the National Committee to continue to coordinate and strengthen cooperation with the Ministry as well as with relevant units to prevent and crack down on human trafficking. Ministry of Tourism requires accommodation business owners nationwide to use the "tourism accommodation registration system"
