Song Saran, president of the Rice Federation of Cambodia, said in an interview with Fresh New TV's "Weekly Special Guest" that in recent weeks the fall in farmers' rice prices has been due to a number of factors, including a drop in rice prices. International markets, weather factors have reduced rice yields and affected the quality of rice and the lack of capital to buy rice stocks of mill owners.
To address the problem of falling rice prices, the President of the Cambodian Rice Federation has requested the support of private banks and the Rural Development Bank, especially from the Royal Government, to formulate a policy to have a budget for the collection of rice stocks this harvest. .
Mr. Song Saran stated, "On behalf of the federation, we need: Post-harvest techniques, rice seeds, reserve budget, asking the Royal Government to have a reserve budget of about 100-150 million dollars to help when the members of the federation "There is a shortage of money to buy rice."
"If the government has a reserve package for rice harvesting, working with the federation for all members of the agricultural community to collect rice so that there is no residue or damage to the quality of rice in the fields," he said.
He added that for the rice market in China, he asked the government to negotiate with the Chinese side so that the country can implement the actual order to improve the price of rice.
He said that in order to stabilize rice prices, only the Royal Government can help, so the federation has proposed to the Royal Government through the Rural Development Bank to continue to reduce the budget to stabilize rice prices.
Similar to Mr. Song Saran, Mr. Oknha Phu Puy, Chairman of the Board of Rice Bank, pointed out a number of factors such as international market issues, climate issues, rice quality issues and the lack of funds to procure rice as a reason for farmers to face lower prices.
"We would like to request the Royal Government to have a state budget through the Rural Development Bank of 100-200 million dollars to enable millers to collect rice from farmers," he said.
