Typhoon Kammuri, the 20th typhoon in 2019, has made landfall in the eastern Philippines and is continuing to make landfall in the central part of the country and heading west. The Philippine Meteorological Agency said the storm was moving at an average wind speed of 155 kilometers per hour.
Philippine authorities have warned of extra precautions for landslides and flash floods caused by heavy rains. Meanwhile, before the arrival of the typhoon, 200,000 people were evacuated to safety in the provinces along the route of the typhoon. While there were reports of small-scale damage in some areas, no serious damage or casualties were reported.
The largest international airport in Manila will be closed for 12 hours from 11:00 am to 11:00 pm Tuesday (Manila time), but air travel Will continue as usual in other unaffected areas. In addition, government buildings and schools were closed in the affected areas.
In connection with the SEA Games, some competitions have been postponed, especially on the water sport.
On average, the Philippines is hit by at least 20 typhoons each year, claiming hundreds of lives. The worst typhoon in Philippine history was Typhoon Haiyan, which left more than 7,300 people dead or missing in 2013.
