At the same time, education gives us not only skills and knowledge, but also ways to use knowledge and skills to contribute to making the world a better place by supporting our family, friends and community.
The three winners receive a copy of Koompi laptops and a bonus of 1 million Riel for the first winner, 800,000 Riel for the second winner and 600,000 Riel for the winner. Third prize.
Sek Thida, a third-year Khmer literature student and first prize winner, said, "I am very excited to win the first prize and this writing program gives me and the Khmer literature students many opportunities to Review the lesson. I would like to thank the Royal University of Phnom Penh and Prudential Cambodia for organizing this writing program. ”
Mr. In Matra, Director of the Insurance and Pension Department of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, fully supports the initiative to help improve financial literacy and use of the Khmer language.
"The insurance sector is part of our country's financial system. The life insurance sector offers two special features: savings and protection," he said. "Because our national economy is good, there are a lot of jobs for students and all business sectors are improving."
At the same time, we would like to thank the Royal University of Phnom Penh for providing the opportunity for the insurance company (Prudential Cambodia) to share knowledge with students and encourage them to consider choosing a job in the field of insurance and social work related to insurance to Let the insurance sector grow.
