The confirmation of Sweden was made this morning, November 29, 2019, on the occasion of Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of Cambodia, receiving Mr. Bjorn Haggmark, the new Ambassador of Sweden to Cambodia. Polite at the Ministry.
At a press conference on the outcome of the meeting, Koy Kuong said that in the past, Sweden had provided assistance in demining, but had recently suspended it. Therefore, in this area, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon asked the new Swedish Ambassador to consider continuing to provide assistance in the demining sector.
"The ambassador has written the request to the Swedish government for consideration," a spokesman told reporters.
According to a report by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, since 1992, Cambodia has cleared about 1,800 square kilometers of land. The area of land that has not been cleared is about 1,500 to 1,800 square kilometers.
The Cambodian Ambassador thanked Sweden for its assistance in the areas of governance, education, labor, human rights, demining and assistance to the Khmer Rouge Tribunal. These grants have contributed to the economic and social development of Cambodia.
Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon said the Ministry of Foreign Affairs was ready to continue cooperating with the Ambassador to strengthen and expand relations between the two countries.
In addition, the Ambassador commended Cambodia for playing an important role in sending peacekeepers to other countries in peacekeeping missions as well as demining missions abroad. He considers this a contribution to world peace.
Koy Kuong said that Cambodia and Sweden also discussed trade and tourism. In the past, trade between the two countries was still small, about $ 70 million a year, so the two sides figured out ways to boost trade.
He pointed out that in 2017, the trade volume between Cambodia and Sweden was only 73 million dollars, but in 2018 decreased to 70 million dollars. In particular, only about 15,000 Swedish tourists visit Cambodia each year.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs added that so far the Swedish education sector has provided scholarships to about 730 Cambodian students. In terms of official ODA, from 1992 to 2018, Sweden provided about $ 545 million to Cambodia, focusing on governance, gender equality, human rights, education, labor markets, as well as environmental and climate change issues.
In addition, in the field of human rights, Cambodia and Sweden have a mechanism called "bilateral human rights dialogue." Earlier, the dialogue took place in Stockholm, Sweden.
Mr. Koy Kuong stated that in the field of the Khmer Rouge tribunal, Sweden provided about 13 million dollars, but provided to the international side.
He said that cooperation between Cambodia and Sweden was established in 1961, and this relationship was restored in 1991. The Deputy Prime Minister and the Ambassador pledged to make the relationship between the two countries closer and stronger.
