(Seoul): Energy sources are very important for all countries in the world, especially in industrialized countries, but in recent decades, global climate change is becoming a hot topic everywhere. As a result, South Korea has decided to suspend about 25 percent of its coal-fired power plants nationwide. This is according to the release by CNA on Thursday evening, November 28, 2019.
In addition to taking steps to address the issue, South Korea has also accused China of being the world's largest source of global pollution and has had a devastating effect on South Korea. Through the air. Everyone knows that South Korea is not rich in natural resources, but it can still operate about 60 coal-fired power plants, which can supply about 40% of the country's electricity.
The decision to suspend the coal-fired power plant is unlikely to affect much of the country's electricity supply, as South Korea is entering a severe winter, with demand for electricity declining. Samdech Techo Hun Sen Visits a Traffic Police Officer in Kandal Province at Calmette Hospital Who Was Hit by a Car While Driving
(Phnom Penh): While caring for his mother-in-law at Calmette Hospital, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, on the evening of November 28, 2019, visited a traffic police officer who was hit by a car. He was seriously injured and underwent surgery while performing traffic control duties for the management.
After hitting the police officer, the perpetrator speeded up and ran away, but with the good cooperation of the police, the car was stopped immediately, but the owner of the car escaped. The victim was rushed to a nearby hospital.
After the examination, the results showed that the skull of the victim had bruises and blood clots, fractures of the lumbar spine and broken legs, and now the doctors to rest for 48 hours when the strength to prepare for orthopedic surgery. Legs and skull continue.
