Nearly 200 Cambodian government officials receive EU training on chemical safety
(Phnom Penh): On November 28, 2019, 170 Royal Government of Cambodia officials received two weeks of training on chemical safety-related aspects within the framework of the Center for Reducing Chemical, Biological and Radiation Risks. And the Nuclear (CBRN) of the European Union (EU) Initiative for Excellence (CoE).
A group of trainees has been selected and trained to become trainers so that they can provide ongoing training and best practices to other trainees throughout Cambodia.
The EU Delegation to Cambodia commended the Royal Government of Cambodia for its interest in promoting chemical safety and security in Cambodia and beyond. Because agriculture and the garment industry consume large amounts of chemicals in Cambodia, the government is well aware of the importance of chemical issues and how to properly manage them.
The project covers 10 countries in Southeast Asia and aims to address issues related to chemical safety and security in the areas of law, regulation, prevention and detection, chemical preparedness and response.
The EU Chemical, Biological, Radiation and Nuclear Risk Reduction Project is funded by the European Union and is jointly implemented by the United Nations Regional Institute for Justice and Crime (UNICRI) and the Research Center. Joint Commission (JRC) of the European Commission.
The project is designed with the technical support of international and regional organizations, EU member states and other relevant partners through close, effective cooperation at the national, regional and international levels. The project involves 61 partners in the region and the world.
