Finally! Donald Trump Signs Law Supporting Protesters in Hong Kong (Video inside) (Washington): US President Donald Trump has signed a bill to make pro-democracy protests in Hong Kong "legal" despite the fact that It could jeopardize efforts to end the trade war with Beijing. This is according to the release by CNA on Thursday, November 28, 2019.
The Hong Kong Draft Law on Human Rights and Democracy has been approved by the US Senate and House of Representatives, and through the signing of Donald Trump, it will become a law that requires the State Department to review or certify. Once a year, whether Hong Kong has full autonomy to receive special trade preferences from the United States.
The law also warns of sanctions for human rights abuses, and Tram also signed a second draft law aimed at banning the sale of some weapons to Hong Kong police, including ammunition. Guns, powder, pepper, tear gas and some materials that can be used to crack down on protesters.
"I have signed this bill out of respect for President Xi Jinping and the people of the city," Donald Trump said in a statement issued Wednesday (Washington time). Hong Kong. "This draft law will become law in the hope that China and Hong Kong can reconcile in a friendly manner for peace and long-term prosperity for all of us."
China and the United States are currently seeking an agreement to end the trade war. But the law is expected to undermine the first phase of the trade agreement, while China has repeatedly warned it will retaliate if a draft law supporting Hong Kong protesters is passed. The driver of the Independence Monument roundabout continued, last night, the police stopped another vehicle
