(Mondulkiri): Civil servants of the three forces, monks, students and people throughout the province celebrated the 14th International Day of Remembrance for Road Traffic Victims at Sen Monorom Park, Mondulkiri Province on the morning of the 27th. This November 2019, with the participation of Mr. Svay Sam Eang, Governor of Mondulkiri Province and Chairman of the Road Safety Sub-Committee of Mondulkiri Province.
"Currently, Cambodia is making progress in all areas, including political stability, security, social security and public order," he said. The living standards of the people throughout the country have also improved and national achievements have improved in all areas.
With the growth of all types of vehicles has increased dramatically in recent years, the number of traffic accidents is also a worrying increase, especially the risk of driving under the influence of alcohol. Road accidents are a threat to human life, animals, the environment and damage to public and private property, leaving behind widows, orphans and the disabled.
According to the report of the National Road Safety Committee of Cambodia, in the third quarter of 2019, there were 988 road accidents nationwide, resulting in 488 deaths and 1 injury. 494 people. In Mondulkiri province, in 2019, there were 32 traffic accidents, resulting in 17 deaths, 29 serious injuries and 13 minor injuries.
These are caused by various factors including: human factors, vehicle factors, road factors, traffic violations, drunk driving and intolerance.
