Ministry of Land Management: Construction quality law will be ready to prevent earthquakes
(Phnom Penh): Due to the recent earthquake on the border between Laos and Thailand, the buildings and houses of the people of this country were severely damaged, so the Royal Government of Cambodia is ready to protect the buildings in Earthquake protection through legislation related to building quality improvement.
This is according to the official of the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction on the occasion of the Ministry in cooperation with the United Nations in Cambodia (UN) jointly opened the Cambodian Urban Planning Forum on "Smart, Sustainable and Sustainable Urban Development" on Wednesday. November 27, 2019 at Sofitel Phnom Penh Phokitra Hotel.
"We are ready for legal action," said Meng Bunnarith, deputy director general of the General Department of Land Management and Urban Planning.
According to Meng Bonnarith, the experience in Laos and Thailand seems new, but in Cambodia there are no earthquakes. He said that in terms of laws and regulations that have been prepared in the construction sector, the Ministry has also considered the strength of the building.
The official from the Ministry of Land Management added that more detailed work may be needed to calculate how much of Cambodia's land area may change or face obstacles in the future. The ministry clarifies each layer of soil, how it can be affected by earthquakes.
