Kampot Provincial Administration Allows 20 Days for Tourist Boat at Kampong Bay Canal to Move West
(Kampot): The Kampot Provincial Administration has set a 20-day deadline for tour boat owners along the Kampong Bay or Kampot Canals to relocate their parking lot to the west to facilitate the improvement of the city's beauty and safety during the ceremony. The Sea Festival is also approaching.
This confirmation was made during a discussion meeting between Kampot Provincial Administration, institutions, departments and relevant authorities with a group of tourist boat traders in Prek Kampong Bay in the presence of Mr. Chiao Tay, Governor of Kampot at Kampot Provincial Hall on the afternoon of November 26, 2019. At present, there are 19 tourist boats in Kampong Bay canal and owned by 15 owners.
The provincial governor determined that the boat owners of the 19 boats above should be moved to the boat dock in front of Kampot Provincial Hall until the base of the old bridge, which is 440 meters long, located in Tvi Khang Tbong village, Andong Khmer commune, Kampot city. December 15, 2019.
Mr. Chiao Tai stated that at present, Kampot province has an increasing number of national and international tourists to serve the interests of the people, but the challenges such as order, beauty, hygiene and environment in Kampot are still limited.
Therefore, the Kampot Provincial Administration, as well as the Kampot Municipal Administration, together with the relevant departments, must have the principle of relocating this new location as necessary to develop the province and better aesthetics, expand the scope of ecotourism in the province and strengthen public order and sanitation. And a better environment along the river in Kampot, especially to ensure the safety of tourists on the boat on the river Kampot.
The provincial governor also set the lighting or plumbing at the new tourist boat to serve traders and national and international tourists who come to visit.
The provincial governor also requires boat owners to install airbags, safety jackets for tourists, to have toilets and clean the water environment without pouring dirt into the canal, and the department must continue to issue licenses to address these challenges. The technical aspects of the location as well as the relocation were successful and transparent.
