In addition, the district governor also advised all officials to grasp what the needs of the people. Senior Minister Son Kunthor: Cambodia is a country with strong laws on microfinance institutions in the region
(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Son Kunthor, Deputy Prime Minister in charge of Small and Medium Enterprises and Microfinance, said that Cambodia is a country with strong laws on microfinance institutions in the region.
This statement was made on the occasion of the Senior Minister's participation in the seminar on "Opportunities and Challenges of Microfinance in Cambodia" recently held at the University of Specialties of Cambodia.
During the ceremony, Senior Minister Son Kunthor also spoke about the growth of the microfinance sector, from small to large, from apartments to large buildings and many branches across the country, and some have branches in Foreigners as well.
Through his experience in the microfinance sector since 1993, Mr. Son Kunthor presented two loans that have occurred until today. First, an investment loan in which traders in other provinces borrowed 2 million riels from the establishment to buy and sell duck eggs. The trader reimbursed both the principal and the interest within one year.
The second type is luxury loans, which are available at mobile phone shops, new motorcycles and cars, and the borrowers do not take motorcycles or cars for business, such as transporting guests, they use them for luxury and this loan does not help. Let their income increase. He also pointed out that interest rates, which used to be as high as 60 percent a year, have dropped to 18 percent a year now.
To answer the question, what is the government's strategy for economic development? Dr. Steng Sokhom, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, explained that the Royal Government has a lot of activities to achieve economic development strategy. The four major pillars of key resources for economic development are: natural resources, human resources, financial resources and technology.
Dr. Steng Sokhom continued that agriculture, industry, handicrafts, tourism, fisheries and many other actors that are the driving force and tools to drive all these sectors of economic growth, such as people with skills, technology, finance, water, electricity, roads, ports, airports and so on. .
He pointed out that overall, the Royal Government has a lot of policies, including cooperation with foreign partners, the establishment of schools and the development of education, the establishment and management of financial institutions, development and technological modernization.
In addition, Dr. Steng Sokhom also made a financial comparison is like blood in the human body. Lack of blood is lack of working capital. When Cambodia has human resources, high expertise, modern technology, large and strong industry.
