The 30th ASEAN-ROK Summit kicks off with a joint fight against protectionist politics (Video inside) (Busan, Republic of Korea): The 30th ASEAN-ROK Summit has officially opened at On the morning of November 26, 2019 at the BEXCO Convention Center in the port city of Busan, Republic of Korea. The meeting was warmly welcomed by the President of the Republic of Korea, Moon Jae-in.
The Head of State, Head of the Royal Government, and Secretary-General Lim Chok Huy attended the meeting, while Cambodia was joined by Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, as the High Representative of Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
Addressing the meeting, Moon Jae-in emphasized that cooperation and solidarity between the Republic of Korea and ASEAN are key to addressing current challenges, such as protectionism, transnational crime and industrial revolution. Fourth and so on.
The summit to celebrate the 30th anniversary of ASEAN-ROK relations is the largest diplomatic event to take place during Moon Jae-in's presidency, with a policy toward the South. Gems.
ASEAN is the fifth largest economy in the world. South Korea has strengthened economic ties with ASEAN since the establishment of the Dialogue in 1989.
The establishment of dialogue has boosted bilateral trade between ASEAN and South Korea from the current $ 6.6 billion to $ 160 billion. Mutual investment increased from $ 1.8 billion to $ 10 billion.
Today, ASEAN is South Korea's second largest trading partner and third largest investment destination, and South Korea is ASEAN's fifth largest trading partner.
ASEAN is now recognized as a new global economic player with the world's third largest population of 650 million young people.
On the occasion of the 30th ASEAN-ROK Summit, the leaders of the ASEAN Member States were warmly welcomed by the Korean President and many trade talks were held. Free trade talks between the Republic of Korea and ASEAN member states have been held, and agreements have been reached and signed, with some countries scheduled to sign next year.
According to a Yonhap news release, Indonesia has reached a free trade agreement with the Republic of Korea after seven years of negotiations on steel, automobiles and beer. The Philippines also plans to reach a free trade agreement with the Republic of Korea next year.
In addition, other ASEAN member states have signed agreements and memoranda to strengthen trade and other cooperation with the Republic of Korea.
