Electricity is extracted from many sources, including dams, coal-fired power plants, fuel-fired power plants, biogas power plants, nuclear power plants, and solar power plants. Generates wind power.
Of all the power plants, the dam is considered the world's purest power plant. Pure because the process of generating hydropower does not harm the environment and is part of flood management, costs less than solar and wind, and dams have a longer service life. The average age is up to 100 years.
And all those projects, some pre-planned, some half-years, some almost a year. "It is a matter of pride that our ministry has selected a company that has the technical capacity, the financial resources and the human resources to do this work and succeed until now."
More than the amount of electricity from other power plants that Cambodia has only 1,060 megawatts. According to economic expert Dr. Ky Sereyvath, in general, each country is always trying to find low-cost energy sources that can produce many types of energy, such as nuclear power plants. But apart from the geographical human resources, Cambodia does not have the power to build a nuclear power plant.
Therefore, Cambodia can not put nuclear power plants along the border, and even more so can not put in Cambodian waters. Because it can affect relations with neighboring countries, affect tourism and have a high risk on environmental safety.
"So hydropower is an important factor in the development of electricity in Cambodia. "So hydropower is very important for Cambodia, because hydropower is only for construction, and after construction, we drain the water, only the management system, we will be able to produce electricity without spending a lot of time." Dr. Ky Sereyvath added that.
