The agreement has been negotiated by South Korea and Indonesia for more than seven and a half years, with both sides on Monday signing a joint statement at a bilateral meeting on the implementation of the Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement. South Korea - Indonesia (CEPA). In addition, according to the South Korean Ministry of Commerce, Seoul and Jakarta will formally sign the agreement in early 2020 after review.
Once the CEPA is signed, it still needs to be ratified by the parliaments of the two countries, and once the agreement enters into force, 93 percent of the Indonesian market will be open to South Korea. It will also abolish tariffs on steel, cars, auto parts and textiles, while South Korea will exempt bunker C and beer imported from Indonesia.
Cambodia, South Korea sign joint statement to study possibility of bilateral free trade agreement
(Busan): The Royal Government of Cambodia and the Government of South Korea have agreed to begin a study on the possibility of establishing a bilateral free trade agreement.
In fact, on November 25, 2019, after a series of consultations between the technical officials of the two countries on the procedure for negotiating a free trade agreement between the two countries, Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce of Cambodia and Ms. You Korean Minister of Commerce Yoo Myung-Hee has signed a joint statement on the start of a feasibility study on the establishment of a Cambodia-Korea bilateral free trade agreement.
The signing took place on the occasion of the 30th Anniversary of ASEAN-Korea Relations Summit and the Mekong-Korea Summit on November 25-27, 2019 in Busan, Republic of Korea. This is according to a press release from the Ministry of Commerce, which was received by Fresh News this afternoon.
The Ministry of Commerce said, "It is expected that the Cambodia-Korea Bilateral Free Trade Agreement will further strengthen relations and trade volume between the two countries, as well as create domestic jobs, export services and expand investment." "Which will contribute to the development of the national economy and benefit the people of both countries."
