Minister of Environment Holds a Meeting to Strengthen the Management and Conservation of Multi-Use Areas and Phnom Samkos Wildlife Sanctuary in Battambang Province
(Battambang): Mr. Say Sam Al, Minister of Environment, in the morning of November 24, 2019, chaired a meeting on "Strengthening the Management of Samlot Multi-Use Area and Phnom Samkos Wildlife Sanctuary" at Samlot District Hall, Battambang Province.
The meeting was attended by Mr. Uy Sotheavy, Deputy Governor of Battambang Province, Mr. Thou Phea, Deputy Governor of Pelin Province, Mr. Ser Samet, Governor of Samlot District, and also with the participation of officials of the Ministry of Environment and many local authorities.
Mr. Say Sam Al said that the meeting focused on preventing and suppressing illegal encroachment and land acquisition in Samlot Multi-Use Area and Phnom Samkos Wildlife Sanctuary in Samlot District, Battambang Province.
At the same time, the Minister will examine and participate in solving some of the challenges that have been occurring related to the management and conservation of natural resources, as well as participate in the development of protected communities to improve the livelihood of the community through consideration. Infrastructure, including clean water projects, provides knowledge on animal husbandry so that communities can better manage their livelihoods.
It should be noted that Samlot Multi-Purpose Area is geographically located in Battambang and Pailin provinces with a total area of 60,000 hectares, and Phnom Samkos Wildlife Sanctuary is located in Battambang, Pursat and Koh Kong provinces. Size 335, 750 hectares. Face-to-face artists gather to form "Voice Recorder Friends Association"
(Phnom Penh): After the society has achieved complete peace under the win-win policy led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, the whole of Cambodia has been becoming the most attractive destination for investors. Invest in food business in Cambodia. The film industry, both domestic and imported, has also been considering Cambodia as a potential competitive market.
Film is the seventh major art form, combining the six major arts: song, dance, painting, sculpture, architecture and literature, which were first brought to Cambodia in 1909. But movies of that era were shown only "pictures and gestures" because there was no sound yet.
It was not until 1927 that researchers discovered the use of sound accompanied by images and gestures that fueled this art form to be highly effective in reflecting the views and ideas of writers who wanted to present and present. The title of the movie is "Pictures, Gestures and Sounds".
Film is the art of "images, gestures and sound", so the producers of foreign films, especially imported films to be screened in Cambodia, need two separate types of artists to participate: the type of performing artists and the type of recording artists.
Of the two types of artists, most viewers see only the performing arts, while the recording artists are less familiar with and familiar with their well-being. . That is why they are often nicknamed "Hidden Artists."
