To Nimol, who fled to Thailand after believing in the incitement of the former CNRP leader and after admitting his guilt, gave an interview to Fresh News on the afternoon of the 24th This November 2019, expressing regret and describing what Mr. Eng Chai Eang has ordered in the past, especially regarding the attempted coup.
A former CNRP activist claims he has been following in the footsteps of Sam Rainsy since 2012, and when the CNRP was dissolved by the Supreme Court, he fled to live with his wife in Thailand. In 2017, receiving and providing money from Mr. Eng Chai Eang $ 200 per month (two wives).
In connection with the coup plot, Mr. To Nimol revealed that Mr. Eng Chai Eang had ordered him to post and share on social media content that was fabricated to pollute the information. Apparently, a letter from the Cambodian People's Party related to the EBA was made by Mr. Eng Chai Eang and asked him to post and distribute it.
He claims that most Cambodian workers in Thailand only care about their work, not what Sam Rainsy and his cronies call for.
After the betrayal plan failed and there was a green light from the Royal Government of Cambodia under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Mr. To Nimol decided to return to his hometown and confess what he had done wrong to the point of loss. Pay time and lose a job due to escape.
Mr. To Nimol further stated that so far, many of Sam Rainsy's supporters have returned to their hometowns and all of them claim to have stopped shooting and cheating on this person because their promises have never been fulfilled. Only repeated scams are true. He added that the CNRP activists are now divided and do not know where to go.
After describing his mistakes, Mr. To Nimol and his wife raised 10 fingers to thank Samdech Techo Hun Sen for his generosity and unforgiveness to him and his wife and others who used to follow in his footsteps. Wrong and promised that he would not do anything wrong again.
The body of Mr. Samreth Ek Sovann has been transported to his hometown for a traditional ceremony
Mr. Samrith Ek Sovann passed away on the afternoon of November 22, 2019 in Chonburi Province in a hospital in Thailand. Expressing his deepest condolences, Huy Vannak, as president of the Union of Media Federations of Cambodia, decided to set up a funeral committee to receive the body from Thailand for a traditional family funeral.
Mr. Samrith Ek Sovann has been involved in the media for many years, with his expertise as a videographer. Through this career, he has contributed a lot to provide information on the activities of national leaders and the development of society. This career also gave him the opportunity to do many different activities, including humanitarian activities.
Especially when Cambodia and Thailand clashed in 2008 in the area of the Temple of Preah Vihear, Mr. Samrith Ek Sovann was a journalist who regularly visited the area to take pictures to inform the Cambodian people about the development. Of the situation at that time.
Currently, Ek Sovann is the main breadwinner in the family while his wife is still at home caring for four small children. Ek Sovann's death is a threat to his livelihood and makes it difficult for him to find ways to support his family.
Kampong Cham Provincial Governor: Road accidents endanger people and animals while developing countries
(Kampong Cham): The Governor of Kampong Cham Province, Mr. Un Chanda, said that while our country is developing on the road infrastructure, traffic accidents are a factor that endangers the lives of people, animals and damage to many private and public property. .
The provincial governor said so on the morning of November 24, 2019, on the 14th International Day of Remembrance for Victims of Road Traffic Accidents in Batheay Village, Batheay Commune, Batheay District, Kampong Cham Province.
"As our country develops both urban and rural road infrastructure and the growth of all types of vehicles in recent years, the number of traffic accidents has increased," he said. It is also worrying, especially the risk of drivers under the influence of alcohol.
Mr. Un Chanda said that traffic accidents endanger the lives of people, animals, the environment and damage to public and private property, not to mention the widows, orphans and the disabled. On the other hand, the loss of large amounts of wealth, if not seriously affecting the national economy, poverty reduction policies and development in all areas of society.
According to the governor, traffic safety is a public health issue that the main development work needs to be addressed urgently, while the number of deaths due to traffic accidents in the world is still high and will increase sharply to about 1, 35 million people every year. For Cambodia, road accidents across the country as of the third quarter of 2019 increased by 988 cases, resulting in 1,494 deaths.
At the same time, the provincial governor called on all departments, relevant units, authorities at all levels, the private sector and people at all levels throughout Kampong Cham province to increase cooperation in the implementation of traffic laws to turn action plans into action. Practical and highly effective. Pailin Governor Calls on Road Users and Passengers to Obey Traffic Laws to Contribute to Prevent and Reduce Accidents
