(Phnom Penh): Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Interior and Chairman of the National Committee for Road Safety, this morning, November 24, 2019, issued an order for relevant institutions to clearly show the number of traffic accidents. There must be no concealment.
Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng emphasized that it is necessary to show this figure clearly to the public, especially those media institutions, in order to jointly reduce traffic accidents, which is an urgent issue that must be addressed together, not an exclusive task. Of any ministry or institution.
On the 14th International Day of Remembrance for Road Traffic Victims this morning, Prince Sar Kheng stated, "We see this trend of increasing traffic accidents as a bad omen. Assess that failure also strikes, but please call for help "Everyone is not an individual."
In response to this, Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng suggested that the National Committee for Road Safety and the provinces should study more clearly and in more detail to better understand the increasing trend of traffic accidents. He said that in the past, Cambodia had received incentives from the United Kingdom to reduce traffic accidents, but in the past two years failed due to the increase in traffic accidents.
According to figures from the National Police, presented by Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng in the first nine months of 2019, there were 3,159 traffic accidents nationwide, resulting in 1,521 deaths and 4,721 injuries. On average, more than five people are killed and 17 injured each day.
Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng also claimed that the main cause of most traffic accidents was due to drivers disobeying traffic laws, causing accidents by speeding 37%, disrespecting the right 24%, disrespecting the right 12%, Overtaking 9%, turning 8%, drunkenness 6%, vehicle factor 3% and drowsiness 1%.
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Steng Sokhom, Chairman of the Board of the Cambodian University of Specialties (CUS), said that the financial sector seems to be blood in the human body, if people have enough blood and good flow will make our body grow well. And finance in the economy has the same pattern.
Mr. Steng Sokhom said this at a seminar on the opportunities and challenges of microfinance held on the afternoon of November 23, 2019 at the University of Specialties of Cambodia (CUS) under the chairmanship of Mr. Sun Kunthor. The delegation accompanied by the Prime Minister, the President of the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce and Industry, and the President of the Microfinance Institution, professors and students.
Mr. Steng Sokhom said that in the economy, finance is considered like blood in the human body, in the human body, if the blood circulation works well, the organs in the body will grow and health will be good. On the other hand, if there is a lack of blood, it will cause the organs of some parts to be blocked, the health is not good. In the economy as well, if any sector is in trouble, it also becomes an obstacle to the rise of economic growth.
He said that the so-called economy is a combination of all sectors of industry, trade, agriculture, services, tourism, transportation are all economies, so if any sector is in trouble, it will hinder the economy. . The economy is also talking about money, and when it comes to money or resources, it is about saving, investing, spending and wasting money.
Regarding the theme of the workshop, the Chairman of the Board said that the Cambodian microfinance sector has made a significant contribution to reducing poverty and improving people's living standards as financial services have expanded. Significantly reaching rural Cambodia, the additional capital reaches low-income people who need capital to run small businesses.
He claimed that the Cambodian financial sector has shown strength in 2018 through strong growth in the size of loans and deposits, while loans that customers do not repay on time remain low. The volume of loans in Cambodia's financial sector, which includes loans from banks and microfinance combined, totaled $ 24.5 billion by the end of 2018, up 19 percent from the end of 2017.
Dr. Steng Sokhom said that there are a number of important factors that accelerate the growth of the microfinance sector in Cambodia, including the policy of support and encouragement of the Royal Government. Regulatory framework and careful oversight of the National Bank of Cambodia. The vibrancy and innovation of microfinance operators and the needs and support of clients, recognizing the importance of the microfinance sector in promoting the financial environment.
Dr. Steng Sokhom said that the microfinance sector has grown significantly in both size and scope. Of course, this progress would not have been possible without a conducive environment for peace, political stability and macroeconomic stability, especially the reform of public financial management, which has improved the management and implementation of policies. Fiscal policy of the Royal Government.
In that ceremony, Dr. Steng Sokhom also raised the question of what measures the Royal Government has to reduce poverty and the income of the people? He said that in fact, the Royal Government has worked hard to develop the country by rebuilding infrastructure, roads, bridges, hospitals, schools and many other areas that are all for the benefit of the people.
In order to be able to absorb the benefits of the Royal Government's efforts, knowledge and ability are required, so our main factor is to require young students to strive to increase their knowledge to become capable people. With the opportunity to grow microfinance, the participating experts also highlighted some of the challenges that have caused people to have distrust of microfinance and for some people not to repay microfinance.
At the same time, the main challenge is that the microfinance sector seems to be more competitive as the number of microfinance institutions continues to increase, while the total population of Cambodia is only about 16 million. To date, there are more than 70 microfinance institutions licensed by the National Bank of Cambodia, and about 200 credit operators.
It is noteworthy that the microfinance sector in Cambodia has seen impressive growth compared to other ASEAN countries.
On that occasion, also raised questions from students to a group of microfinance experts related to lending, savings, capital increase, targeted use of loans, interest rates that are still high, Asking questions related to the Royal Government's measures to help the poor in rural areas, etc.
The experts also explained in detail and gave good ideas to all students to save money rather than take the money to pay for the purchase of unnecessary items such as installment of smartphones or motorcycles. Recently, new cars to show off while they do not have the savings as an asset for themselves.
These services are provided to customers in order to facilitate and improve the financial situation of families as well as businesses, and to continue to expand the network to cover the local level across 25 capitals and provinces.
