A report by the Ministry of Labor claims that Cambodian workers working in Korea work in three main areas: agriculture, industry and construction. They can earn from $ 1,500 to $ 2,000 and some more than $ 3,000 a month.
They can save and send money back to improve the livelihood of families in Cambodia, an average of about $ 1,000 per month. Through high wages from working in Korea, Cambodian workers have been able to help solve various family problems and also help build a better family life.
Chum Soyy, 32, from Boribo District, Kampong Chhnang Province, has been working at an aluminum factory in Sasang, South Korea. He has been working in Korea for about six years.
Sui considers the opportunity to work in South Korea to be a scholarship to study, gain experience and knowledge to improve life, and he considers the salary from that work as a price for his scholarship.
Mr. Soyy earns up to about $ 3,000 a month, and most of it is saved and sent to his family in the district. Sui described with a smile that wages from working in Korea have improved his life, and he has gained a lot of work experience and knowledge.
"Honestly, family life is now more prosperous than before, and I personally have gained a lot of experience and knowledge in this district, including travel, food and communication with Koreans." This is Mr. So Yi's confirmation.
Another young Cambodian, Bin Samet, 30, from Rolea Bier district, Kampong Chhnang province, has been working in Korea for more than seven years. Currently working at Hyundai Automobile Factory.
Seeing that his family's life was difficult and he did not want his family to support his education after the bankruptcy, Samet, the eldest son in the family, decided to study Korean and come to work in Korea after passing the bankruptcy exam in 2010.
Through a high salary of more than $ 2,000 a month, Samet helped his family improve his living conditions and helped his younger brother go to college. Samet considers this an honor for a family in a village with few college students.
Bin Samet stated, "I came to Korea first, we studied in Korea, second, we got income from our salaries, which helped my four younger brothers to graduate and graduate from college." . Thus, my family in the district has a family honor. In my village, it is a remote village with all the bankrupt family members so far. On the other hand, parents are happy because we have helped support the children and family.
A Cambodian woman from Tang Kork district, Kampong Thom province, Chan Srey Mom, 31, a former factory worker in Cambodia, worked hard to learn Korean until she passed the exam and came to work in Korea during 2013. Ms. Mom came to work in Korea with the expectation that working in Korea would help improve her living conditions with higher wages. Srey Mom currently earns between $ 1,800 and $ 1,900.
Ms. Chan Srey Mom claims that in the past six years, she has saved about $ 80,000 and helped build a house for her parents and created a business for her parents.
"It was very different when I was in Cambodia as a factory worker, the wages were not as high as in Korea," she said. "Since I came to work in Korea, my family life is very different."
The Royal Government considers job seeking abroad, especially in Korea, as one of its efforts to provide people with jobs and improve their lives. But there is still criticism from some people that the government is incapable of creating jobs for the people and pushing people to work as laborers in other countries.
Mr. Soyi said, "If we did not come, if the Samdech did not allow, did not sign or there was no agreement between the Republic of Korea and Cambodia, we would not have been able to come." Thus, because he is a leader, he has a good relationship and he seeks markets for people to work, livelihood or other skills.
Ms. Chan Srey Mom said, "For me, you came to work in Korea can be said to be lucky because coming to work here earns a lot of money can help family life. Thank you to the Royal Government for sending me, as well as other workers who came to work, they are happy, but happy is not a punishment for us, because working here pays a lot and some of them get skills. "More."
There are many Cambodian workers after coming to work in Korea, which gives them the opportunity to save money and other knowledge and use the money and knowledge from Korea to start a business in the country. Mr. Sokhy, Mr. Sameng and Ms. Mom have similar ambitions.
The three have claimed that they will not be working in Korea forever and are currently planning to start a business in the country to start a new life in their homeland.
Cambodian Ambassador to the Republic of Korea Long Dimanche claims that about $ 600 million a year is sent to Cambodia through Cambodian workers in Korea. According to Long Dimanche, this flow of wages is also contributing to the national economy.
Mr. Long Dimanche added that working in Korea not only helps Cambodians get higher wages. But also contributes to the development of technical skills for them.
People from 16 countries have been allowed to come to work by the South Korean government. Cambodians are assessed as hard-working and well-liked by their employers. This is according to Mr. Dimanche.
Seeing the benefits of working in Korea, the Royal Government of Cambodia is also seeking the Korean government to allow more Cambodian workers to work in the country.
