(Phnom Penh): At the Second Cambodia Tourism Investment Forum under the theme "Determining the Stages for Growth", Mr. Thong Khon, Minister of Tourism, said that the Royal Government of Cambodia is opening more opportunities for the private sector, investors to invest and Expand investment in ecotourism.
In relation to this investment, the Royal Government of Cambodia has just announced a national ecotourism policy that provides an opportunity for investors to increase their investment.
Under the PPP mechanism, the state can provide assets such as islands, natural lakes, seas, national parks or state-owned assets to private investment partners for investment, and the proceeds of the investment are divided with the state under an agreed contract.
He informed the ceremony that the Royal Government has issued a sub-decree on the establishment of sacred tourism sites, focusing on the development of major tourist destinations in Cambodia.
To prepare for receiving 15 million tourists by 2030, the Royal Government plans to improve existing tourism products, diversify new and unique tourism products and destinations with enhanced quality of tourism products and services, security, safety, social order and Develop tourism human resources with a focus on skills.
In particular, more attention should be paid to the creation of sacred tourist sites, clean tourism cities, smart and green cities, attractive tourism, coastal-island tourism, leisure and sports centers, children's parks, amusement parks, wildlife tourism, 4-star and 5-star luxury hotels. High-end restaurants, shopping malls, etc.
Investment in infrastructure such as roads, tourist ports and airports in a number of tourist destinations is fundamental to the development of tourism in line with the Industrial Revolution 4.0.
The Ministry of Tourism and related institutions have also been focusing on the Coastal-Island Development Project as a model for high-end and green ecotourism, continuing the efforts to manage Cambodia's beaches, developing the environment sustainably and responsibly.
