(Siem Reap): The Brahma Kel temple is an ancient hospital built by Jayavarman VII, built of sandstone in the late 12th century, dedicated to Mahayana Buddhism. Today, the temple is in a state of disrepair, with part of the temple wall in the southwest corner collapsing and almost completely collapsed.
In order to strengthen the temple to avoid risks, the APSARA Authority has organized the first phase of the resistance process from November 20, 2019 to February 2020.
Mao Sokny, director of the Brahma Kel temple restoration site and architect of the Department of Temple Conservation in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology, said the risk of damaging the temple structure was due to the surrounding stone pillars.
He said that the main front door was tilted unbalanced and the west wall was tilted and the lower wall was not supported by the southwest corner of the temple wall. The south is prone to collapse due to the loss of many rocks below.
The expert said that because the south-west side of the temple rock, one side of the temple collapsed in the southwest corner and the rest unstable, so its top is at high risk, urgently needed to stand firmly to keep the temple standing strong.
In addition, he stated that there is one new risk point that needs to be addressed urgently, and there are four old points that need to be resisted to strengthen and tighten three cables at the top of the temple.
The same expert said that the first step is in accordance with the ICC-Angkor recommendation in May 2019, which visited and recommended the second step is to study the general blueprint, excavation research on the structure of the temple and steps. Third, to repair the temple.
