An urgent statement issued by Fresh News on November 20, 2019 stated, "Countries along the Lower Mekong will experience severe drought from now until January 2020. "Thailand and Cambodia will be hit harder than Laos and Vietnam."
The warning came after preliminary data analysis by the Mekong River Commission showed that the drought was caused by insufficient rainfall during the rainy season due to the late monsoon rains and the early cessation of monsoon rains. Both the El Nino phenomenon, which causes abnormally high temperatures and evaporation.
Monsoons usually begin in late May and end in October, but this year they begin to fall almost two weeks late and stop about three weeks ahead of schedule.
Dr. Lam Hong Son, Director of the Regional Center for Drought and Floods of the Mekong River Commission Secretariat, stated that "Prolonged drought this year could have a negative impact on crops and agricultural products, as well as water shortages." "If the drought continues."
According to the Mekong River Commission of Oddar Meanchey, Preah Vihear and Siem Reap of Cambodia will be most affected by this drought.
