Asia-Pacific Summit 2019-Cambodia adopts eight joint statements
(Phnom Penh): The Asia-Pacific Summit 2019-Cambodia, which was held in Cambodia on November 19, 2019, issued eight joint statements.
The Asia-Pacific Summit is organized by the Global Peace Federation (UPF) in collaboration with the Kingdom of Cambodia, the Asian Cultural Council (ACC), the Civil Society Forum (CSAF) and the International Association of Parliamentarians for Peace (IAPP). Former and current heads of state and government, presidents of parliament, ministers, members of parliament, including religious organizations, civil society, youth and the media.
This Joint Statement adopted the following eight main points:
* First, the international community must take immediate and effective action to address the challenges posed by climate change and other environmental issues that threaten the very existence of our humanity and our planet, which is our only habitat. If ignored, it will pose a direct danger to us.
* Third: Couples and families are essential to achieving lasting peace and happiness as contributing factors to sustainable development and noted in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights: "Natural groups and the foundations of society" that "have the right to protection from society and the state."
* Fourth, the need to enhance the role of women who are victims of domestic violence, discrimination in the workplace, limited access to education, including deprivation of access to opportunities. Government officials at all levels of society have identified the root causes of gender inequality by exploring the harmonious and complementary characteristics of men and women.
* Fifth: The need to include the leadership roles of youth and students by calling on them to participate in the implementation of social obligations, integrity, morality and responsibility in building a culture of peace through the formation of a public mindset. .
* Sixth: The importance of recognizing and respecting successful peace initiatives by the government and the private sector, including individuals, noting the win-win strategy developed by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of the Kingdom of Cambodia. Helped to improve the well-being of the Cambodian people, as evidenced by the Peace Awards and many other forms of public recognition.
Seventh: The ideal of living for the benefit of others is an effective way to help people adapt to differences and overcome divisions in human relationships at the family, social and global levels and can promote reconciliation. Together to create a single global family culture.
The Summit appreciated and noted the comments and suggestions raised by the participants in this Summit Program, especially the recommendations for monitoring and continuing the impetus starting here. All of us who are participants in the Asia-Pacific Summit 2019-Cambodia.
The following is the Phnom Penh Joint Statement:
