According to the report, of the 8 million jobs in Cambodia, 37% are paid jobs, most of which provide high incomes and additional protection benefits to workers. But another 63 percent are still traditional jobs. This includes farming or family businesses, which are less integrated into the modern economy and provide lower wages for workers.
She added that foreign-owned companies have played an important role in creating high quality jobs in Cambodia. By 2015, a third of all paid work in Cambodia was in foreign-owned companies. During 2010-2015, the garment industry was the fastest growing industry, increasing its share in job creation by 1.1 percent per year.
Ms. Ingonado Pracha said that local companies outnumber foreign-owned companies, but they do not contribute much to job creation. Local companies employ an average of eight workers per company, compared to 124 in foreign-owned companies.
The World Bank Country Manager for Cambodia added that one of the major concerns is to ensure that Cambodian workers have the necessary skills so that they can compete with workers from other countries in search of jobs in management companies. By foreigners. In 2016, 37.6% of exporters said that the uneducated workforce was the main obstacle to doing business.
According to Wendy Khan and Hem, senior economists and report authors, "The success of Cambodia's work strategy will depend on the involvement and cooperation of all stakeholders in all sectors of the economy, not just the makers. "Not only government policies and leaders, but also entrepreneurs, investors, development partners and especially the workers themselves."
