Legal Awareness: Affecting Individual Freedom
(Phnom Penh): In today's human society, every person is given the right and complete freedom of communication as well as daily living without restrictions. However, the exercise of individual rights and freedoms must be subject to the rule of law, and the exercise of one's rights and freedoms must not infringe upon the rights and freedoms of others.
Infringing on the freedom of others that is against the law will be held accountable for its actions before the law. Of course, when an individual suffers an abuse that violates his or her freedom, the perpetrator will be held accountable for his or her actions.
But what kind of actions are defined as a violation of individual freedom? And what kind of responsibility will the perpetrator have?
To find out more about this's legal awareness program, Fresh News, in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, would like to raise the topic of "Affecting Freedom" to the following students:
The word freedom is the ability of people to do something according to their desires and preferences, or in other words, a way that people are not bound by any conditions or constraints. Meanwhile, Samdech Chuon Nath's dictionary writes that "Freedom is a power that can do whatever it wants, but under the law, full power (but there are also basic legal articles)."
The Constitution of the Kingdom of Cambodia also states that the Cambodian people are equal before the law, have all the same rights, freedoms and duties, regardless of race, color, sex, language, religion, religion, or political affiliation. Nationality, social status, resources or other status. The exercise of individual rights and freedoms must not infringe on the rights and freedoms of others. The exercise of this right shall be in accordance with the provisions of the law.
In general, freedom is the full exercise of the power of the individual, but can not be used beyond the law, which can affect the rights and freedoms of others, otherwise the perpetrators will be held accountable before the law.
The Penal Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia also clearly defines a number of notions relating to acts that infringe on the freedom of others, as provided in Chapter 5 on "Infringement on Freedom."
Chapter 5 of the Penal Code identifies two offenses that infringe on the freedom of others, the first of which is illegal detention and detention, which stipulates in Article 253 that a person arrested Detain another person without an order from a legal authority or other than as permitted by law to be punished:
In addition, the penalties for both offenses will be aggravated if the above two offenses are found to be aggravated.
In short, all freedoms are fully guaranteed and are protected by law, which in principle does not allow any person to commit any act that infringes on the rights and freedoms of others.
The provision of laws to punish the violation of the rights and freedoms of others is really an important rule to ensure a society of mutual respect and value.
