General Chuon Sovann: The Golden Triangle remains a dangerous source of drug supplies that requires a stronger fight
(Thailand): General Chuon Sovann, Deputy Director of the National Authority for Combating Drugs of Cambodia, on November 15, 2019, led a delegation from the Cambodian Drug Control Agency to attend the Ministerial Meeting of the signatory countries of the Memorandum of Understanding. 1993 MoU on Drug Control in the Greater Mekong Subregion in Bangkok, Thailand.
General Chuon Sovann continued that in response to the complex situation, Cambodia has made efforts to increase the effectiveness of the fight against drugs both in its country and in the sub-region to participate in the implementation of sub-regional action plans to contribute to the prevention of production. And the trafficking of all kinds of drugs from the Golden Triangle to other countries in the region and around the world.
He added that at the same time, Cambodia has been actively implementing the fourth multi-sectoral anti-drug campaign to improve people's well-being, maintain security and social order, and prevent the production and trafficking of illegal drugs. Laws in our region as a whole, and Cambodia has already cracked down on drug trafficking across the country in many cases and in large quantities of hundreds of kilograms.
At the same time, in order to enhance the effectiveness of the fight against drugs in our region as a whole, Cambodia believes that:
Attention should be paid to strengthening existing mechanisms and the development of new mechanisms in line with the new situation of the drug problem in our region. * Cooperation should be further promoted More work to control drug offenses, especially in each area and at each border. * More attention should be paid to preventing the production and trafficking of drugs from the Golden Triangle.
General Chuon Sovann emphasized that we should continue to make more efforts to mobilize financial and technical support from donors and development partners to further support our sub-regional action plans.
At the meeting, member states of the Memorandum of Understanding on Drug Control expressed concern over the increase in production and trafficking of synthetic drugs by organized crime groups. Much can be done by diverting chemicals from legitimate sources to the production of illicit synthetic drugs.
Therefore, in response to this issue as well as current drug trends, the Meeting decided to issue the Bangkok Declaration on the Effective Response and adopt the 11th Sub-Regional Action Plan.
It focuses on cooperation in cross-border drug control, prevention and suppression of production, trafficking and trafficking of all kinds of illegal drugs and chemical substances by land, air and sea, as well as strengthening Strive to treat drug addicts with technical and financial assistance.
