(Preah Vihear): The Minister of Environment, Mr. Say Sam Al, signed an official letter to Mr. Prak Sovann, Governor of Preah Vihear Province, on the possibility of transferring the duties of Mr. Ear Sokha, Director of the Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Environment to return to work at the Ministry of Environment. .
According to the official letter signed by Mr. Say Sam Al on November 6, 2019, which was received by Fresh News on the afternoon of November 14, 2019, the content informed Mr. Prak Sovann to examine the possibility of Transferred Mr. Ear Sokha, Director of Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Environment to return to work at the Ministry and appointed Mr. Song Chan Socheat as Acting Director of Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Environment.
It should be noted that from October 25, 2019 to November 6, 2019, Mr. Say Sam Al has signed the removal of two officials of the Department of Environment. The officials include: Mr. Vun Thoeun, Director of the Kulen Prom Tep Sanctuary Office of the Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Environment, and Mr. Ear Sokha, Director of the Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Environment. China opens talks on free trade agreement with Cambodia as EU warns Cambodia over EBA
(Phnom Penh): As the European Union completes its preliminary report on the suspension of the preferential tariff system (EBA) on Cambodia, submitting this report to the Royal Government of Cambodia for review and response on November 14, 2019, the Embassy of the Second World Power. China has released new information on the official opening of free trade talks with Cambodia, which will begin for the first time in early December.
Cambodian Ministry of Commerce spokesman Seang Thai told Fresh News by telephone on the afternoon of November 14, 2019 that the Ministry of Commerce has prepared a delegation to leave for Beijing, China to hold formal talks on Free Trade Agreement led by a Secretary of State of the Ministry of Commerce.
To the question of how long will it take to achieve a free trade agreement with China? Mr. Seang Thai claimed that bilateral talks are easier than regional talks due to the good relations between Cambodia and China, so it is expected that the talks will be completed as soon as possible within a year or two. The next year or three.
However, the spokesman did not elaborate on the principles of the Cambodia-China bilateral free trade talks, but said they were based on mutual trust.
"When we did not have a free trade agreement with China, we signed a sanitary and phytosanitary agreement one by one, but hopefully when we negotiate, it will be successful," he said. "Hopefully, the agreement will be packaged so that we can export more to China."
The EBA issue has been used to put pressure on the Royal Government of Cambodia, arguing that the country is experiencing human rights abuses, but the Royal Government of Cambodia has rejected and declared that even if the EBA is lost, Cambodia will not die. The European Union will formally decide in February 2020 whether or not to suspend Cambodia's preferential treatment.
In the draft budget for 2020, the Royal Government of Cambodia has set aside more than $ 170 million to be used to prepare measures to respond to the impact of uncertainty regarding the preferential trade system with a number of partners.
Please be informed that the Royal Government of Cambodia and the Government of China are scheduled to hold the first formal talks on the feasibility study of a free trade agreement between the two countries in early December.
The Chinese Embassy in Cambodia considers the opening of the first formal talks on the feasibility study of a free trade agreement between the two countries to be a special arrangement of the Chinese side based on the friendly relations between China and Cambodia.
Please be informed that the trade volume between Cambodia and China, which in 2018 reached 7.4 billion US dollars, of which Cambodia's exports to China amounted to 1.377 million US dollars, an increase of 37% and imports were $ 6 billion, an increase of 26%. The two countries have set a new goal of boosting bilateral trade to 10 billion US dollars by 2023.
