The same source confirmed that in these 36 traffic accidents, the causes of traffic accidents include: speed, disrespect, right-handed, competition and turn. Cambodian Representative to the United Nations warns that the Special Rapporteur is not a judge with the power to adjudicate the release of anyone
(Phnom Penh): The Permanent Mission of Cambodia to the United Nations Office in Geneva has issued a statement in response to a statement by Rhona Smith, Special Rapporteur on the situation of human rights in Cambodia, demanding the dismissal of Accused of Mr. Kem Sokha.
With this two-page statement, the Cambodian Permanent Mission to the United Nations Office reminded that the Special Rapporteur is not a judge with the power to adjudicate the release of anyone.
The Cambodian Representative to the United Nations stated: “The Cambodian Mission would like to remind the Special Rapporteur that his mandate is to assist Cambodia in the promotion and protection of human rights through dialogue and cooperation based on The basis of the principle of neutrality, impartiality, fairness, non-partisanship and non-political transformation. "The Special Rapporteur is not a judge who has the power to adjudicate the release of anyone."
The Cambodian representative explained that in Cambodia, the separation of powers between the executive and the judiciary, which are independent institutions, is set out in Article 51, Article 128 and Article 130 of the Constitution of the Kingdom of Cambodia. Such activities are not conducive to strengthening the rule of law and law enforcement in Cambodia.
The Cambodian representative said that the amendment to some of the conditions of the court's examination of Mr. Kem Sokha was the result of respecting the court's past obligations and based on humanitarian grounds related to his health condition. However, Kem Sokha will receive a fair trial after the investigation is completed.
In Cambodia, violators will be prosecuted and prosecuted on the basis of solid evidence, not because the perpetrators belong to any group. Offenses are offenses and cannot be used as an argument for any other purpose. Such practices are the same in other democratic societies. This is according to the statement of the Permanent Mission of Cambodia to the United Nations.
