Book Distribution Program on Genocide and Massacre in World History
(Phnom Penh): The Documentation Center of Cambodia, in collaboration with the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, is proud to announce a public program to distribute the book "Genocide and Massacres in World History: An Overview in the Cambodian Classroom."
Although the five case studies have different approaches to explaining the history of genocide, these case studies all encourage reflection, comparison, and discussion.
Each chapter begins with a brief lesson with minutes to aid teachers as a reference for reading, Q&A, and discussion. Each chapter also includes in-depth information, "detailed lessons" that teachers can learn through questions and topics, as this activity requires a lot of information and time for discussion.
Youk Chhang of the Documentation Center of Cambodia believes that this book will make a significant contribution to Cambodia's efforts to evolve from a society plagued by genocide into a courageous society. Confronting its past as well as setting an example for other countries in the future.
"Cambodia cannot escape its history, but Cambodia must not be a slave to its own history," Youk Chhang said. The future of Cambodia is not only determined by its past struggles and self-healing, but also by helping other nations, nations and the world to fight and heal. Avoid massacres and unreasonable extremism.
