If traveling on National Road No. 6 from Phnom Penh to Kampong Thom from July to December at the point of Sankor B village, Sankor commune, Kampong Svay district, we have observed that most of the people here are engaged in hitting ambok with a mortar and pestle. And what we see different from other areas is that the mortar of the people here hit with a bulldozer instead of using human force to step on the mortar, unlike in the past.
Not only that, even the roasting of rice before hitting and sifting husks from the husks is done by the people here using machines. This has enabled the people living in Sankor B village to produce more ambok to meet the daily needs of customers. Due to this innovation, the people in this area, on average, they can hit 40 to 50 kg per day, depending on the needs of customers.
As for the recipe of hitting the ambok of the people in Sangkor village, Sangkor commune, Ms. Khon showed that there are two types of delicious ambok according to the type of rice. As for the rainy rice that is used to make the most delicious ambok, it is Rumduol rice because it has a sweet and thin aroma and is easy to eat.
About how to beat the ambok to have a delicious smell, taste and hygiene, they have to choose fresh rice, then wash the rice with water and then dry it in a frying pan, the first two pans have a role Drying the rice The second pan is used to grind the rice until it is cooked before hitting it. To know that the rice is cooked, one has to observe the color of the rice that is being roasted in the pan that it turns black and starts to explode a lot.
Back to talking about the method of eating ambok, usually most Cambodians know that ambok can be eaten by mixing it with coconut water, coconut meat, palm juice, banana, ice cream or sugar. But Ms. Khon said the other way is to roast the ambok with the spice and add a little soup powder to add flavor. Another way to do this is to roast the ambok with dried apricots and dry it. This way, they can keep the ambok for a long time and have a delicious taste.
