Ministry of Tourism helps many former workers and migrants to study tourism and get jobs in the country
(Siem Reap): The Ministry of Tourism this morning, November 6, 2019, presented its success in implementing a pilot training program on tourism skills for many former migrant workers and young people who have a tendency to return to work abroad. Study tourism skills and get a job in the country.
The above dialogue highlights the achievements and experiences of implementing a pilot project on training migrant workers returning to Cambodia and young people wishing to migrate to work in tourism in line with ASEAN competency standards.
Currently, a total of 60 former workers from Thailand and young people from poor families (36 women) have received both theoretical and practical training on hotel services at the Pol Dubrul Hotel and Tourism School in Siem Reap for 3 months. Internship at a hotel for 3 months in Thailand.
As a pilot from 2018, with the support of the Swiss government through the International Organization for Migration, 60 young people have graduated, 35 of whom will soon complete internships in Thailand to return to work in the country.
The program will continue by providing more skills to young people to provide skills to ex-workers returning from Thailand and young people who have no skills and intend to migrate.
(Phnom Penh): The Phnom Penh Capital Administration on November 9th announced to the people and business owners some guidelines related to traffic avoidance as well as other information during the Royal Water Festival, floating lanterns and worshiping God. Ok Ambok month, which will take place from November 10-12.
The points that the Phnom Penh Capital Administration introduced include:
2. Prohibit heavy trucks, cement trucks, trucks from entering Phnom Penh and suspend all construction sites in Phnom Penh from 9 to 13 November 2019.
3: All vehicles come to visit the Royal Water Festival, float lanterns and worship the moon, Ok Ambok, no traffic on Tonle Sap Road, Street 130, Street 5, Street 154, Norodom Blvd., Street 214, Street 51, Street 294, Samdech Sothearos Blvd. to the corner of Russian Embassy Street, National Assembly Blvd., Street 78, Sobert Street, Preah Suramarit Blvd.
4: On National Road No. 1: Trucks and trucks passing through Phnom Penh must park in the area of Wat Niroth Rainsy, Chbar Ampov parking lot (Suy Sophan bus stop) and divert heavy traffic at the corner of National Road No. 1 Tiger Factory Road. All types of passenger cars and tuk-tuks must be parked in the Niroth Rainsy pagoda and along Street 369 (south of Wat Prek Pra).
5: On National Road 2: Trucks and trucks must be parked at Chak Angre Krom car park and in Chak Angre Secondary School. For all kinds of passenger cars and motorbikes, trailers must park at Chak Angre Krom parking lot and in Chak Angre Secondary School compound, Chak Angre Leu pagoda courtyard. For heavy vehicles traveling on Street 217, stop north of Dey Krahorm roundabout.
6: On National Road No. 3 and National Road No. 4: All kinds of transport vehicles passing through Phnom Penh to the northwestern and northeastern provinces must travel on Victory Boulevard along National Road 5 or across the Prek Pnov Bridge. .
Trucks and trucks must be parked at the Chom Chao parking lot in front of Phnom Penh International Airport and at the 2004 traffic light. For cars and motorbikes, tuk-tuks transporting passengers to visit the Royal Water Festival, floating lanterns and worshiping the moon, Ok Ambok must stop not to exceed the traffic light.
9: On Street 271 and Street 371: Trucks and trucks are parked south of the Mall Bridge. For cars and motorbikes, tuk-tuks transporting passengers to visit the Royal Water Festival, floating lanterns and worshiping the moon god Ok Ambok must stop at the pig nose at the head of the road.
10: On Chom Chao Street (Veng Sreng Street): Passenger cars and tuk-tuks entering Samdech Preah Monireth Blvd. must park at Stung Meanchey Primary School. Heavy vehicles must avoid traffic at Stung Meanchey Pig Nose.
11: For cars and motorbikes, tuk-tuks transporting passengers to visit the Royal Water Festival, floating lanterns and worshiping the moon Ok Ambok on Street 598 and Oknha Mong Reththy Blvd. Kim Il Sung Street will be parked at the Boeung Kak Lake Development Zone.
The following is the announcement of Phnom Penh Capital Administration: Minister of Tourism: To combat climate change, we must develop community-based tourism and ecotourism.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Thong Khon, Minister of Tourism, said that in order to combat global climate change, people must work together to protect the forest environment as well as natural resources in a sustainable way and reap the benefits through tourism, including community tourism. And ecotourism is at the core of sustainable development.
Minister Thong Khon made the remarks at a review workshop on the third model tourism competition 2019 at the Phnom Penh Hotel on November 6, 2019.
Through this, the Minister continued that while protecting the forest environment, cultural resources and natural resources, people can create a tourism community by earning more than hunting, logging or subsistence farming.
He said that when the community has a rich diversity of resources, along with more attention to hygiene, quality of service, food safety and good hospitality, the community itself will receive more and more tourists and the presence of tourists, which has brought revenue flow. Funds flow into the community continuously, solving daily life easily.
He encouraged the people, public and private institutions and development partners to continue to participate in the protection of the forest environment, cultural resources, natural resources in a sustainable way to jointly reduce and combat climate change on our planet.
More importantly, the participation of the community in the above work also contributes to the implementation of the principles of sustainable development of the World Tourism Organization, that is, not only for the present, but for the future.
Mr. Thong Khon added that Cambodia has northeast, southwest and some areas have potential for ecotourism and community tourism. Apart from that, the cultural tourism area of the temple also has important potential for the development of community tourism.
