Pyongyang: North Korea has condemned the United States after Washington described Pyongyang as a "state sponsor of terrorism" in its annual report. This is according to a quote from Sputnik on Tuesday afternoon, November 5, 2019.
According to the report, North Korea was considered by the United States as a "state sponsor of terrorism in 1988" for the main reason that North Korea was involved in the 1987 bombing of South Korean Air. In 2008, the North Korean name was removed, but was re-incorporated by the United States in 2017.
North Korea has agreed to join talks on denuclearization efforts on the Korean Peninsula with the United States since 2018, but things have fallen into disarray after the second summit between US President Donald Trump and the United States. North Korean leader Kim Jong Un ended in defeat in Vietnam in late February 2019 due to disagreements over sanctions on North Korea.
Last October, talks between North Korean and US officials in Sweden did not seem to yield any positive results, as North Korea wants the United States to stop pursuing provocative policies.
