(Phnom Penh): Article 17 of the Law on Associations and Non-Governmental Organizations has been proposed by civil society organizations to the Ministry of Interior for amendment in order to supplement what is lacking for them in practice and to comply with Evolution in today's social context.
In fact, on the morning of November 4, 2019, the Ministry of Interior held a meeting to discuss and consult on the proposals and recommendations of civil society organizations related to the above amendments. The meeting was chaired by Mr. Bun Hun, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior and Chairman of the Royal Government Working Group in Response to Civil Society Requests.
Speaking to reporters after the meeting, Mr. Bun Hun said that during today's meeting, the Ministry wants all representatives of civil society organizations to raise various arguments that they want to be amended, but for this morning, the group The work has just reviewed only four articles, while these four articles, the civil society itself is not yet clear that they want to correct.
Seeing this, Mr. Bun Hun requested that about 5,000 civil society organizations properly registered with the Ministry of Interior hold a joint meeting to identify more specific goals in order to facilitate discussions in Later, as well as expedite the proposed amendment.
Mr. Bun Hun further stated that the 17 articles that the association proposed to amend focus on the general provisions, reports, neutrality of all political parties and some other points. As for the Royal Government's position on proposing amendments to this law, Mr. Bun Hun affirmed that the Royal Government always welcomes the proposals of associations that are properly registered in the Ministry of Interior.
In response to the suggestion that the Law on Associations and Non-Governmental Organizations has just come into force, but why amend it? Mr. Bun Hun explained that what is set out in the law also evolves according to the social situation, because some laws are not only in Cambodia, sometimes just done and not yet implemented, must be amended. In particular, the proposed amendment is not a big deal.
Please be informed that this Law on Associations and Non-Governmental Organizations was approved by the National Assembly on July 13, 2015 at the 4th Session of the National Assembly, 5th Legislature, and the Senate fully reviewed the form and concept of this law on On July 24, 2015, during the 3rd Plenary Session of the 3rd Legislature, as announced by the Constitutional Council in accordance with the Constitution in Resolution No. 156/004/2017, dated 12 August 2015.
This law aims to protect the right to freedom of association and non-governmental organizations in Cambodia in order to protect its legitimate interests and protect the public interest, as well as to promote cooperation in partnership between NGOs and non-governmental organizations. Yes, the government and the public authorities.
To date, more than 5,500 local civil society organizations have been properly registered with the Ministry of Interior, excluding foreign associations. On average, there are about 20 associations that have applied for a post-law list of associations and non-governmental organizations.
