(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation (MoFA) has released the official results of the 35th ASEAN Summit and related summits on November 2, 2019, which were attended by Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation. Participated in the 20th Meeting of the ASEAN Political-Security Community Council and the 24th Meeting of the ASEAN Coordinating Council.
According to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the 20th meeting of the ASEAN Political-Security Community Council discussed traditional and non-traditional security issues that affect the recent developments in the areas under the ASEAN Political-Security Community and the progress of Implement the detailed plan of the ASEAN Political-Security Community 2025.
Regarding the challenges and future plans for the implementation of the detailed plan of the ASEAN Political-Security Community, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon stressed the serious impact of cyber security and the need for joint efforts to combat cybercrime. Cross-border crime, especially drug smuggling.
Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon briefed the meeting on the contribution of the ASEAN Center for Mine Action (ARMAC) to raising awareness of the dangers of landmines and explosive remnants of war in the region and the world. He also briefed the meeting on the outcome of the Regional Workshop on the Implementation of the ASEAN Women, Peace and Security Agenda held in Phnom Penh in August 2019.
In particular, the 24th Meeting of the ASEAN Coordinating Council discussed the role and operation of the ASEAN Secretariat, the implementation of the Action Plan of the Initiative for ASEAN Integration (IAI) and the progress of the implementation of the ASEAN Connectivity Master Plan (MPAC) 2025. .
In his intervention, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon thanked the Indonesian government for its commitment to ASEAN for the recently inaugurated ASEAN Secretariat in Jakarta.
For IAI, Prak Sokhon reaffirmed the importance of this initiative in promoting regional integration and encouraged to focus more on improving the competitiveness of small and medium enterprises. Promoting public-private partnerships to deepen digital integration and capacity building programs.
Regarding Timor-Leste membership in ASEAN, he stated that Cambodia supports Timor-Leste as a member of ASEAN and welcomed the positive developments through the fact-finding mission conducted in September 2019. He also encouraged the Economic Council and the Socio-Cultural Council to conduct a fact-finding mission in Timor-Leste as soon as possible by early 2020.
