(Bangkok): The United States and the European Union are discussing withdrawing economic benefits from Cambodia in response to what happened in the 2018 general election. The ruling party won all the seats in the National Assembly after the opposition was dissolved by the Supreme Court and disqualified from party leadership.
According to the West, Cambodia's elections are not democratic. But strangely, despite this, no protests were seen at all, and on the contrary, the general public seemed to be relieved when the election process was over.
I visited Cambodia as the Minister of Commerce and Industry of Singapore in 1999. On the way to Angkor Wat, Cambodian Minister of Commerce Cham Prasidh pointed out where Prime Minister Hun Sen survived a rocket attack a few weeks ago when I visited. Cambodia at that time was not completely safe, while weapons were everywhere.
A few years later, I asked Prime Minister Hun Sen about his attempt to take his life, and he told me that he knew who ordered it, but he would not respond because the main purpose His greatest goal at the time was to find peace.
The United States has also recently expressed concern over the development of a new Chinese-sponsored port and airport in Sihanoukville, which could turn into a Chinese military base. As an appropriate response, Cambodia has announced that it welcomes the investigation because the Cambodian government wants to show that it has nothing to hide.
In turn, due to the factors of peace and increased ASEAN cooperation by accepting Cambodia, the country's economy has benefited from the growth of tourism, production and agriculture. Cambodia is located between Thailand, Vietnam, Laos and the sea, which is an important strategic transportation location. No country in ASEAN wants Southeast Asia to be a war zone again, including Cambodia.
What Cambodia has done at least in the last 20 years is amazing. However, Cambodian friends should remember that the country is still in a fragile development process. We should help this country move forward in the right direction and not make this process more difficult.
According to many Western perspectives, most ASEAN countries do not even live up to their high standards. It cannot be achieved overnight, but it is also not right if someone wants to weaken these countries. Of course, it would be great if the West supported ASEAN, a non-aligned and economically vibrant region.
