Air in New Delhi 'poisoned' as authorities close schools
NEW DELHI: India's capital, New Delhi, is facing another crisis: air pollution has soared, forcing authorities to temporarily close all schools until May 5. November as city dwellers are advised to try to stay indoors or always wear masks when walking down the street. This was released by CNA on Friday evening, November 1, 2019.
As the entire capital was polluted by smog, plant fires in several states bordering New Delhi were sending plumes of smoke across northern and New Delhi. The fires were claimed by Indian authorities to have been caused by hot, dry weather that has swept across northern India.
According to Indian officials, the situation is likely to ease in another week. The closure of the school is to prevent children from being exposed to toxic fumes as authorities increase the number of devices to monitor air pollution levels.
