BEIJING (Reuters) - China and the United States have maintained close ties over talks to defuse trade war, with the first phase of a deal likely to be finalized in the middle, the Chinese Foreign Ministry said on Friday. November. This is according to the release by CNA on Friday evening, November 1, 2019.
China's claim is no different from that of the United States, as US President Donald Trump on Thursday announced that the two countries will announce a new venue for a joint meeting to sign a trade agreement. The "first phase" comes after Chile decided to postpone the APEC summit, scheduled for mid-November.
The Chinese Foreign Ministry wants the meeting between China and the United States (possibly between Chinese President Xi Jinping and US President Donald Trump) to be held in Macau, but the United States has not. No response has yet been made on China's options. The time for the meeting is likely to be in mid-November.
