Senior Minister Osman Hassan tells Indonesian students that Cambodia has peace and development under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen
JAKARTA - INDONESIA: Senior Minister for Special Mission Osman Hassan, Special Envoy of the Organization of Islamic Cooperation (OIC) and a member of the Supreme Council of the World Islamic Council (RABITA), has said that peace and prosperity in Cambodia can be achieved under leadership. Right of the Royal Government of Cambodia and the contribution of Friends Indonesia.
This statement was made on the occasion of Senior Minister Osman Hassan giving a lecture on the topic of national harmonization and the political situation in Cambodia at Al-Azhar University, Republic of Indonesia on the morning of October 31, 2019, which was attended by a large number of leaders and students. Crowds.
Senior Minister Osman Hassan continued that after many years of war, a nation with only empty hands under the leadership of the Royal Government with Samdech Techo Hun Sen as Prime Minister has turned this country into a trapped country. The world's seventh fastest-growing economy will grow by more than 7 percent over the last 20 years.
In addition, the Royal Government of Cambodia has included 1,500 Khmer-Muslim teachers as civil servants who have been paid by the Royal Government since 2015. These are the efforts of leaders at all levels, in which the Islamic community is also a part of the commitment to unite as a whole to follow the right policies of the Royal Government.
Senior Minister Osman Hassan added that Indonesia has also played an important role in contributing to the peace process in Cambodia, both during the negotiations and the Paris Agreement, expanding cooperation with Cambodia, helping to train troops and develop Human resources in Cambodia have contributed to sending peacekeepers (UNTAC) to Cambodia to provide scholarships to Cambodian students, including Cambodian Muslim students.
In addition, the Republic of Indonesia has co-sponsored the establishment of the Cambodian Muslim Intellectual Alliance to join the ASEAN Muslim Intellectual Alliance. In particular, the Cambodian Khmer Muslim community knew Indonesia well through its cultural, traditional and religious ties, and considered the Republic of Indonesia a good friend.
The Indonesian side continued to push for the provision of legal documents, exchange of experience and training on technical management procedures and co-operation in the areas of halal industry standards, pilgrimage, Zakat management (donations), Islamic education system and pledges to continue scholarships. For Cambodian students to continue their studies at universities in the Republic of Indonesia.
According to MABIMS statutes, member countries must not interfere in each other's political affairs, as there is an annual biennial meeting of senior officials and ministers of religious affairs, with Indonesia as chair and host.
(Kampong Speu): Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara, Minister of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction, presides over here this morning a ceremony to hand over real estate ownership certificates to concessionaires of the second phase of the Land for Social and Economic Development (LASSED II) project in the commune. Reaksmey Samaki, Oral District, Kampong Speu Province.
Mr. VER Samnang, Governor of Kampong Speu Province, said that the Land Allocation for Social Affairs and Development Phase II, also known as LASSED II, is a project that supports the implementation of the Royal Government's Social Land Concession Program to distribute land to the landless and The poor through social land concessions to improve the livelihoods of poor and vulnerable families.
The main achievements in the implementation of the project from 2016 to 2019 was a total budget of 403,717.11 dollars. In addition, in collaboration with development partners such as GIZ, to improve the livelihoods of concessionaires, improve land use as much as possible, improve people's well-being and ensure sustainable food security.
GIZ supports and responds to a number of concessionaire needs, such as the construction of water wells, the provision of agricultural equipment and supplies, zinc, tractors, and community capacity building training, as well as the provision of solar rice seeds. Currently, there are a total of 566 land concessionaires in Reaksmey Samaki commune, of which 444 are residential and agricultural land, 115 are residential land and 7 are agricultural land.
On that occasion, Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara described the gathering as a celebration of the great success of LASSED II, a political program of social land concessions of the Royal Government of Cambodia, with the support of the World Bank and the international community. The Ministry contributes to the success of this project.
Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara noted that the first success: more than 80% of the people who came to live on the land in this project, they live permanently, and the second success: the authorities are aware of the well-being and needs of the people. Citizens in need.
